WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.117 --> 00:00:02.066
[hisses]

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.430
Certainly don't try to suck out venom,

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:09.800
because that's never really been shown

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.490
to improve the outcome of any snakebite.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:13.770
I'm Dr. Cyrus Rangan.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.200
I'm a medical toxicologist

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.110
with the California Poison Control System,

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.780
where I've been assistant medical director

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.370
for the past 20 years.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:23.400
Today we'll be looking at
toxicology scenes in movies

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.588
and judging how real they are.

00:00:25.588 --> 00:00:27.685
Who the h--- are you?

00:00:29.651 --> 00:00:31.310
Hail Hydra.

00:00:33.193 --> 00:00:35.770
What it looks like is
that he has a false tooth

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.757
that actually contains cyanide.

00:00:37.757 --> 00:00:41.470
The concept of actually hiding
cyanide in a false tooth

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:44.330
seems to be a little bit more
folklore than it is fact.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.030
And in fact, when you talk to people

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.070
at the National Spy Museum,

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.580
they'll tell you that
this is not something

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.140
that people really engaged in.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:55.000
But did villains maybe back in, you know,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.120
the time of Nazi Germany,
things like that,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.000
did they use cyanide
capsules to commit suicide

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.150
so that they would not
be incriminated later?

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:04.680
Yes, that practice actually did occur.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.680
There still are some problems with this.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.860
The concept of the foaming
followed very quickly by death

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.842
is probably more theatrical
than it is expected symptoms

00:01:13.842 --> 00:01:16.170
that you would see from
a scientific standpoint.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.430
Is it possible that someone might have

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.170
some excessive drooling or foaming

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.210
while they're experiencing other symptoms

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:23.530
of cyanide poisoning?

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:24.960
Sure, that's possible.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.610
But for that to be the only symptom

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.340
and then so quickly followed by death,

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.430
that's what gets a little
bit far-fetched here.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.660
Cyanide, although it is a
relatively fast-acting poison,

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.860
meaning it doesn't take hours to kill you,

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.750
it still does take a few minutes at least.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.278
And perhaps in that five-
to 10-minute period,

00:01:42.278 --> 00:01:44.500
that's when death generally is seen.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.720
I could give this somewhere
between maybe a 3 or a 4.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.320
Qyburn here is the cleverest man I know.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.047
Clever enough to learn
what poison you used

00:01:52.047 --> 00:01:53.588
to murder Myrcella.

00:01:53.588 --> 00:01:54.680
The Long Goodbye, was that it?

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.027
Qyburn: The Long Farewell.

00:01:56.027 --> 00:01:56.860
That's the one.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.360
Well, obviously, we are
not aware as toxicologists

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.180
of any poison that is even
nicknamed The Long Farewell.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.810
So it sounds like it's entirely made up.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.520
Cersei: How long does the poison take?

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.561
Qyburn: Difficult to say. Hours, days.

00:02:09.561 --> 00:02:11.690
It depends on the subject's constitution.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:12.920
But death is certain?

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.060
Qyburn: Oh, yes, your
grace. Quite certain.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.720
If we are to go with his assessment

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.050
that this could take several hours

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.270
or perhaps days to kill you,

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:23.970
there are some toxins out in the world

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.330
that could act like that,

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:29.050
and the one that comes to
mind is the disease botulism.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.080
And botulism is a disease that does work

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.238
relatively slowly from that standpoint.

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:44.980
The concept of putting
it on somebody's lips

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:46.030
and kissing that person

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:48.260
and then being able to neutralize that

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.360
with some kind of an oral antidote

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.660
is a little bit far-fetched.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.680
When it comes to poisons
and antidotes or antitoxins,

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.760
it really depends on the poison itself

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.130
in terms of whether there is an antitoxin

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:02.420
or an antidote available.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.280
It's not common that we would
have a very deadly poison

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.680
that you could take immediately,

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.710
and then give an antidote
immediately orally

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:13.900
that is suddenly going
to neutralize that poison

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.330
and limit its ability to kill you

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.550
if it's something that
would kill you otherwise.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:18.880
For this one, I would probably put it

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.853
in the range of about 2 to 3.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:23.090
I smell nothing.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.370
What you do not smell
is called iocane powder.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:28.200
It is odorless, tasteless,
dissolves instantly in liquid,

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.610
and is among the more
deadly poisons known to man.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:34.220
For Westley to say that
it dissolves instantly

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:36.390
in any liquid is also a
little bit far-fetched,

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.300
because, for example, things
that dissolve well in water

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.860
don't dissolve well in
alcohol and vice versa.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:44.330
So to say that it dissolves in any liquid

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:48.222
is something that probably goes
beyond the realm of science.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.130
We're talking about a poison here

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:59.030
that kills someone very, very quickly,

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.616
and there aren't really many
poisons that can do that,

00:04:01.616 --> 00:04:03.210
that can kill you within seconds

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.193
where you look just fine
and then suddenly die.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:09.190
I spent the last few years building up

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:11.040
an immunity to iocane powder.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.640
In general, when we're
talking about poisons,

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.033
these aren't things that you can really

00:04:15.033 --> 00:04:17.060
build up an immunity towards.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.780
You can build up immunity
towards infectious diseases,

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:22.190
but building up an
immunity towards a poison

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:24.660
is not something that we generally see.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.970
There is a concept
that's called tolerance.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:29.520
So, for example, with drug users,

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.270
they tend to require
higher and higher doses

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.290
of whatever drug of choice they use

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.240
in order to achieve their desired effect,

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:37.960
and that's a different kind of concept.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.870
But the concept of immunity from a poison

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.740
is not something that we
would consider scientific.

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:44.115
I would probably give it a 3.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.233
Peeta!

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.140
That's nightlock, Peeta!

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:51.928
You'd be dead in a minute!

00:04:51.928 --> 00:04:52.761
I didn't know.

00:04:52.761 --> 00:04:56.362
So, nightlock is fictional as
far as the name is concerned

00:04:56.362 --> 00:04:59.485
and probably is derived from nightshade,

00:04:59.485 --> 00:05:02.080
and perhaps they're
combining it with hemlock,

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:03.390
which is another poison.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.260
But when you look at
those particular berries,

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:09.070
those look like what are called
Atropa belladonna berries,

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:11.710
which is commonly referred
to as deadly nightshade,

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:14.500
where you could get
dizzy, kind of confused,

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.970
a little bit loopy, and
possibly unresponsive.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:19.720
And sometimes patients can be so severe

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:21.490
that they get seizures and coma,

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:23.550
and if you're going to die
from deadly nightshade,

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:24.480
that would be the reason.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.210
Where it does fall apart
a little bit for me

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.760
is when she says that it
will kill you in a minute.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.660
The bottom line is that you
should never, ever, ever

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:34.810
do your own foraging
unless you are a botanist

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:37.070
and someone who's well trained.

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:39.727
I'd probably put it around a 4.

00:05:39.727 --> 00:05:41.596
[hisses]

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:45.987
When I was a kid, whenever we went hiking,

00:05:45.987 --> 00:05:48.338
we always carried olive
oil and a razor blade

00:05:48.338 --> 00:05:50.010
in case of a snakebite.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.730
The depiction of this
particular snakebite wound

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:55.060
looked a little bit unusual to me.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.109
Usually, a snakebite is
a subcutaneous injury,

00:05:58.109 --> 00:06:02.090
where the fangs make
it down into the tissue

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:04.110
at or below the skin layer,

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.670
and then you start to
get a lot of destruction

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:08.500
of the tissue around that area

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.970
and a lot of inflammation
and subsequent swelling.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:11.890
So you don't expect to see

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:15.083
that sort of voluminous bag of fluid.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:18.246
Maria: So brave.

00:06:19.277 --> 00:06:20.750
What we have learned, however,

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:22.450
over the last several decades,

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:24.730
is that cutting into a snakebite wound

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:27.090
is probably a very bad thing to do.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.300
And we've seen many cases where people

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:33.350
actually introduce more
injury into the situation

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:35.290
than help the situation.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:37.520
So people are cutting
into arteries and veins,

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:40.100
cutting into nerves, cutting into tendons.

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:42.350
So these are very bad
things that can happen

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:44.290
as a result of slicing into that wound.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:46.931
So in general, we don't
slice into snakebite wounds.

00:06:49.816 --> 00:06:50.649
Ah!

00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:52.961
Mom: Good boy.

00:06:52.961 --> 00:06:54.810
And we certainly don't
try to suck out venom,

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:56.160
because that's never really been shown

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:58.760
to improve the outcome of any snakebite.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:01.670
In medieval times, people back then said

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:03.640
that you should coat
your lips with olive oil

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.370
and open the wound and suck out the venom.

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:08.360
And, again, this was
back from medieval times,

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.270
but unfortunately, a lot of
those kinds of treatments,

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:12.853
especially for envenomations,

00:07:12.853 --> 00:07:14.950
carried over through the century.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:17.150
The general rule of
thumb for any snakebite

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:21.880
is simply to get to a hospital
as soon as humanly possible.

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:24.130
There really is no other first aid

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:25.080
that you really need to do

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:26.750
other than to keep the patient calm

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:28.212
so you can be evaluated

00:07:28.212 --> 00:07:30.140
and evaluated for the need for antivenom.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.160
I would actually rate
the accuracy quite low,

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:33.610
somewhere between 0 and 1,

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:36.370
and simply because they are
talking about treatments

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:39.215
that could really make
a bad situation worse.

00:07:39.215 --> 00:07:40.763
Castor beans.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:44.000
Jesse: So what are we gonna do with them?

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.202
You're going to process them into ricin.

00:07:47.202 --> 00:07:49.960
Ricin, of course, is a real toxin

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:51.240
derived from natural sources.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.120
It actually comes from the
castor bean or the castor seed.

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:56.180
When you're ingesting ricin,

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:59.740
it causes mayhem in your
gastrointestinal tract.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:01.318
So what's going to
happen is you're going to

00:08:01.318 --> 00:08:05.620
experience extremely
voluminous fluid loss,

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:09.730
and it can be so severe that
it's impossible to catch up

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:12.270
by just simply drinking and
trying to replace those fluids.

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:13.310
And can it cause death?

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:15.370
Yes, it can, from all those fluid losses.

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:17.090
Walt: It's toxic in small doses.

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:20.090
Also fairly easy to
overlook during an autopsy.

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:21.970
And that's probably true, that if someone

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:26.470
was experiencing extreme
vomiting and diarrhea

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:29.410
and dehydration and then death,

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:31.260
ricin is not necessarily the first thing

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:32.240
that people would think of.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.181
People would think more
about infectious diseases

00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:36.880
causing those kinds of problems.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:39.270
Walt: Kind of under the weather?

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.011
Like you've got the flu?

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:43.890
That would be the ricin I gave you.

00:08:45.103 --> 00:08:46.350
Oh, my God.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:49.000
There is no direct antidote for ricin.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:50.060
It's really, the treatment

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:51.970
is what we call in
medicine supportive care.

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.750
So in other words, we
treat the bad symptoms

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:58.150
that occur from the
actual poisoning itself.

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:01.913
You know, she was on the phone
and conscious and, you know,

00:09:01.913 --> 00:09:04.120
so if she was experiencing symptoms,

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:06.410
she probably was in the very early phase.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:09.330
So she probably could
get herself to a hospital

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:11.530
and maybe get ahead of the poisoning

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:14.270
by making sure that she's
got IV fluids on board

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:15.230
as soon as possible

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:17.480
before the severe symptoms took place.

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:20.210
Yeah, it's entirely possible
that he's giving it away

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.020
a little bit too early for her

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.550
so that she could potentially
get treatment and survive.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:26.170
As far as the scientific accuracy,

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:27.303
I would give this an 8.

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:30.350
Dealer: And fold.

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:36.200
It begins, of course, with Bond

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:38.650
drinking a poison from his glass.

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:42.370
What we learn later is that
this poison was digitalis.

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:45.610
Now, digitalis is a real poison.

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:46.635
It's a real compound.

00:09:46.635 --> 00:09:48.743
But digitalis itself is available

00:09:48.743 --> 00:09:50.090
in a number of different plants.

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:51.870
So there's a plant called foxglove,

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:53.220
another one called lily of the valley.

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.720
It's actually a medication,

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:57.270
and it helps the heart
pump more efficiently.

00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:59.410
And when given under medical supervision,

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:03.013
under precise circumstances,
and we're checking drug levels,

00:10:03.013 --> 00:10:05.740
it's a reasonable thing
to do for patients.

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:07.790
However, in large doses,

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:10.356
digitalis and other related compounds

00:10:10.356 --> 00:10:12.339
can cause severe poisoning.

00:10:12.339 --> 00:10:16.310
Some poisons can cause some major symptoms

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.640
with just a very, very small dose.

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:20.812
Digitalis probably would
not be one of them.

00:10:20.812 --> 00:10:23.090
I would not expect digitalis in the form

00:10:23.090 --> 00:10:25.650
of a couple of drops in a martini glass

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:27.200
and then after that is then, you know,

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:28.890
distributed throughout
the rest of the glass

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:30.190
and then a couple of sips later

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:32.920
you're suddenly experiencing
digitalis poisoning.

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:34.262
That's a little bit far-fetched.

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:45.020
And while it is true that if you instill

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:48.670
a concentrated salt solution
into your stomach very rapidly

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:50.290
it will likely make you vomit,

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:54.250
making somebody vomit after
taking a poison into the stomach

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:57.970
is actually not well
supported scientifically.

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:01.810
So this is not really a practice
that we espouse anymore.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:03.700
So that's something where he was probably

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:05.440
really wasting his time.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:06.490
Bond's been poisoned.

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:08.040
He's going into cardiac arrest.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:09.883
Stay calm.

00:11:09.883 --> 00:11:12.230
Ventricular tachycardia. Digitalis.

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:15.150
So now they have identified
the toxin in record time.

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:17.380
Now, that is a real
term, and that's a term

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:20.150
referred to the lower
chambers of the heart

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:22.800
uncontrollably beating very, very fast,

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:25.530
and that is something that does
require immediate treatment.

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:28.410
One thing that I could
say that they represented

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:30.526
more or less effectively

00:11:30.526 --> 00:11:32.851
was the feelings that he was having,

00:11:32.851 --> 00:11:34.370
the symptoms that he was having.

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:36.510
So it sounds like he was having

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.770
a pounding feeling in his
chest and some nausea,

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:40.000
and he's sweating.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.260
Those are the kinds of symptoms

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:44.130
that you might expect
from digitalis poisoning.

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:45.930
Take the blue combi-pen, Bond.

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:48.009
Mid-neck into the vein.

00:11:48.009 --> 00:11:49.763
That will counteract the digitalis.

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.800
They have him inject
something into his neck.

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:55.970
The term "combi-pen" is something that,

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:57.410
it's a simply made-up word.

00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:00.770
I'm just hazarding a guess that
lidocaine is what they used,

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:03.980
because that is something
you can inject into people

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:07.580
who have ventricular
tachycardia from a poisoning.

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:09.940
But as I mentioned before,
it's got to be given

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:12.749
under very well-supervised circumstances.

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:19.182
Now get yourself off to a hospital.

00:12:19.182 --> 00:12:20.173
Bond: I will do.

00:12:21.948 --> 00:12:23.080
As soon as I've won this game.

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:25.230
If you have any poison on board

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:27.920
that's causing an abnormal heart rhythm,

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:31.180
yeah, you want to restore the
heart rhythm back to normal,

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:33.300
but that doesn't cure you of the poison.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:34.610
But what he actually needs

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:36.820
is the real treatment for digitalis,

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:40.340
which is digitalis-specific antibodies,

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:43.340
and that actually is the
first-line treatment.

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:45.210
It is a little bit unbelievable

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:47.390
that he's not rushing
to a hospital right now

00:12:47.390 --> 00:12:51.140
to get that key treatment
that he actually needs.

00:12:51.140 --> 00:12:53.560
Probably give this about a 3.

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:54.940
Jellyfish sting!

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:56.587
Oh, it hurts, it hurts, it hurts!

00:12:56.587 --> 00:12:58.174
Well, there's really only
one thing you can do.

00:12:58.174 --> 00:12:59.512
What? What is it?

00:12:59.512 --> 00:13:00.780
You're gonna have to pee on it.

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:02.520
What I think they really did get right

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:05.280
is the fact that jellyfish stings

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:07.970
tend to impart immediate pain,

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:10.510
and it's quite severe for
a lot of people as well,

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:14.700
depending on how much of the
jellyfish tentacle got on you.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.280
Where I think it falls apart

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:18.140
is when it comes to the treatment.

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:20.833
It's long been folklore

00:13:20.833 --> 00:13:23.980
that you should urinate
on a jellyfish sting.

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:26.340
There is no scientific backing to it.

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:29.080
You simply use the seawater to wash off

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:31.080
any remnants of those tentacles

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.330
that may still be on the site.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:34.460
Those remnants include

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:38.760
tiny little cell-like
bags called nematocysts.

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:41.350
If they remain on your skin,
they can still discharge,

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:43.725
and so we like to use
salt water, essentially,

00:13:43.725 --> 00:13:45.183
to cleanse those off gently.

00:13:45.183 --> 00:13:47.330
So, urine has the potential

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:49.120
to be able to accomplish that too.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:50.777
You're better off just using the seawater

00:13:50.777 --> 00:13:51.800
than peeing on yourself.

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:54.990
If you put fresh water
onto a jellyfish sting

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:57.440
where those nematocysts
are still on your skin,

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:00.750
they can discharge under
conditions of fresh water.

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:04.270
So if your urine perhaps is
too dilute at that moment,

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:06.730
you could actually
discharge those nematocysts

00:14:06.730 --> 00:14:08.180
and cause more pain.

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:10.900
In a lot of cases, urinating on yourself

00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:13.940
probably won't make
things too bad for you.

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:16.870
It probably won't help,
but in certain situations,

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:18.700
it could make things worse.

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:21.540
This is not a good illustration
about what to do afterwards.

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:24.443
I would give this around a 6.

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:29.790
Kitai: Dad, I can't feel my hands!

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:32.030
Cypher: The poison is
affecting your nervous system.

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:33.030
Stay even.

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:35.280
An insect sting or insect bite

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:37.680
that could result in the depression

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:39.330
of the central nervous system,

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:43.350
that's probably not going
to happen from most things

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:48.110
in this sort of immediate
fashion that we're seeing here,

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:50.030
within seconds or within minutes.

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:53.580
There is actually a disease
called tick paralysis,

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:56.290
where there's a certain kind
of tick, a hardback tick,

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:58.710
that actually has a neurotoxic venom

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:01.690
that's injected in very,
very tiny amounts into us

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:04.210
but can actually cause
full-body paralysis.

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:05.830
But the tick has to latch onto you

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:08.520
and stay there for several
hours, perhaps even a day.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:11.240
So there is that real possibility

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:15.290
that, yes, an insect
could give you paralysis.

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:16.900
Would it happen this quickly?

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:17.840
I don't think so.

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:20.430
Inject yourself directly into your heart

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:21.810
with the first stage.

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:22.793
Do it now.

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:27.530
We give the liberty that this
is a science-fiction movie

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:31.090
and that there is some
fictional superbug out there

00:15:31.090 --> 00:15:33.500
that is causing this paralysis.

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:36.457
But would we have a situation

00:15:36.457 --> 00:15:40.010
where we thought a neurotoxin
injected from a bug

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:43.920
would need to be treated by
an injection into the heart?

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:46.600
That's where things fall
apart for me as well.

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:49.730
If you had, for example,
an allergic reaction

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:51.750
to, let's say, a beesting,

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:54.030
you could still also give
intravenous epinephrine

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:55.040
by putting it into a vein.

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:57.010
But if you didn't have that access,

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:59.200
you didn't have the ability to set that up

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:02.020
and time's ticking away,

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:04.050
you can make the argument
that you could give

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:06.270
an epinephrine shot
directly into the heart.

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:08.280
And that actually is the initial treatment

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:10.330
we would give for nerve-agent exposure.

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:12.470
It would be atropine
directly into the heart.

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:14.340
So, yes, there is an argument to be made

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:17.030
for intracardiac injections
of certain medications

00:16:17.030 --> 00:16:19.950
in very specific circumstances,

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.960
but certainly not for an envenomation.

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:24.520
I'd probably put this in
the range of about 0 to 1.

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:27.130
I'd say the one that's most
scientifically accurate

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:28.500
was the one from "Breaking Bad."

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:31.350
They used good, correct terminology

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:34.050
and gave an illustration of
what is supposed to happen

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:35.950
to a person under these circumstances.

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:38.070
The most fun to watch, I
think, was "Princess Bride,"

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:39.930
as I think it is for most people.

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:42.930
If you enjoyed this video, click
